  ],
  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "6eaf7378-b00a-4ac9-b30b-0d64b4e2d576",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students share something they know a lot about with a small group, building early comfort speaking up and previewing the idea that everyone brings valuable knowledge to this course."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6eaf7378-b00a-4ac9-b30b-0d64b4e2d576",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a7e6bbfe-5ab1-40fb-844c-e712320b275d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a822a33e-4f46-4aba-8998-799f5b6b15b1",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Welcome to Computer Science Principles! This year we’re going to learn a lot about computer science, but before we begin, we want to learn a little bit about you and your thoughts on computer science in general. Let’s start by seeing how creative we can be right now!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a822a33e-4f46-4aba-8998-799f5b6b15b1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a7e6bbfe-5ab1-40fb-844c-e712320b275d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8382bd2b-1f72-493f-9491-a4f74b76d889",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of 2-4. Direct students to introduce themselves while you distribute sticky notes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a sticky note or scratch paper."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8382bd2b-1f72-493f-9491-a4f74b76d889",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a7e6bbfe-5ab1-40fb-844c-e712320b275d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "17569318-ba84-4aae-832c-df66be61f975",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a minute to write down something that they know a lot about or could teach someone. Once students are ready, click the animated slide to reveal the next steps: each person in the group should share what they wrote down on their sticky and then tell the group something interesting about that topic.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for topics that might resurface later in the course, and prompt any hesitant students with a follow-up question about what they shared.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The goal is for students to name something they're interested in and know enough about to share real insight — it doesn't have to be a school subject. Much of their willingness to open up will come from your own genuine curiosity about what they share. If students get stuck, remind them everyone has some hobby, interest, or hidden talent worth talking about."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "17569318-ba84-4aae-832c-df66be61f975",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a7e6bbfe-5ab1-40fb-844c-e712320b275d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "be036253-06a4-4483-862f-95d4c0a858d2",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "be036253-06a4-4483-862f-95d4c0a858d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a7e6bbfe-5ab1-40fb-844c-e712320b275d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aac098b6-5cb1-41b3-9e06-7472ac5017af",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aac098b6-5cb1-41b3-9e06-7472ac5017af",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a7e6bbfe-5ab1-40fb-844c-e712320b275d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "936782d6-9785-4712-97c2-6933844a6762",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students identify how technology touches an area they're interested in and sketch out a creative way it could be improved or a new problem it could solve, practicing the mindset of noticing problems and imagining solutions.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Rapid Prototype an Innovation"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "936782d6-9785-4712-97c2-6933844a6762",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b5e10b6-d3a4-4f30-9237-045096e941be"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c52c88e9-a60a-406b-93ec-93acbe06b8d6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Technology seems to be everywhere, affecting everything we do. But it starts with someone recognizing a problem and building something to solve it. As a class, we're going to try \"rapid prototyping\" — a prototype is just a quick sketch of an idea, the first draft something real might be built from.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c52c88e9-a60a-406b-93ec-93acbe06b8d6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b5e10b6-d3a4-4f30-9237-045096e941be"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c9c6bf86-d68a-4c43-943c-e91d2ee76c59",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a poster paper."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c9c6bf86-d68a-4c43-943c-e91d2ee76c59",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b5e10b6-d3a4-4f30-9237-045096e941be"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "220d7413-3ea2-40d6-b913-fc53865123c8",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have each group work through their members' areas of interest, brainstorming how technology touches each one and one way it could be improved.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that every group member is contributing ideas across all the interests in their group, not just their own.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep things quick. If a group is worried about not being innovative enough, remind them that very small ideas can have big consequences. People once thought it was ridiculous that you would want to send a short text message to another person over a phone.\n\nAlternatively, a group may have a great idea that they want to spend more time on. They can do that later. For now, just remind them it's a rapid prototype."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "220d7413-3ea2-40d6-b913-fc53865123c8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b5e10b6-d3a4-4f30-9237-045096e941be"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2b59f7ac-4b16-45b4-bb22-d986907bae29",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct groups to agree on the idea from their brainstorm that would be most interesting to the rest of the class, sketch it on their poster, and jot down one possible downside or new problem their idea could create.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As groups work, prompt them to think beyond the benefit — ask \"what's one way this could go wrong, or create a new problem?\" if they're stuck.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If a group starts perfecting their drawing, remind them this is still a rapid prototype — a rough sketch is enough. Polish isn't the goal; conveying the idea clearly is."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use the completed posters as a quick formative check — do students' sketches show a technology idea connected to a real interest, and does their noted downside or new problem show genuine thinking about effects, not just a token answer? This can help you gauge how well the class is grasping both lesson objectives before moving forward."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2b59f7ac-4b16-45b4-bb22-d986907bae29",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b5e10b6-d3a4-4f30-9237-045096e941be"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5785bf3d-ef7e-429d-9259-399a19a2ad55",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students put their posters on the wall. Do a \"Gallery Walk\" or a whip around so that each student can see all of the other groups' work.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Take this opportunity to explain the importance of bringing individual interests and perspectives to this course. From day one, students should be thinking about how to apply the principles they learn to their own lives — hopefully with excitement."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5785bf3d-ef7e-429d-9259-399a19a2ad55",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b5e10b6-d3a4-4f30-9237-045096e941be"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e3e847d4-bf1c-48e1-ad83-199575ba593b",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students see how computer science touches a wide range of fields beyond what they might expect, connecting back to the prototyping they just did.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Making Connections"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e3e847d4-bf1c-48e1-ad83-199575ba593b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b5e10b6-d3a4-4f30-9237-045096e941be"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3411c7c6-63f1-4b11-9687-5f1c19553c12",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We're just starting this class, but you all bring passions and knowledge about things you care about — and it's likely that those things involve technology in some way. Everyone's unique, but we're all in this together.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3411c7c6-63f1-4b11-9687-5f1c19553c12",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b5e10b6-d3a4-4f30-9237-045096e941be"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c17ad798-4038-4f7b-9ee0-c1255d25c399",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Show the \"Computer Science is Changing Everything\" video.\n\n**Questions to consider with the video:**\n- What are you excited to learn about in this course?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students can discuss in small groups and then share out with the whole class. It's ok if at this point students don't have an answer to this prompt."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c17ad798-4038-4f7b-9ee0-c1255d25c399",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b5e10b6-d3a4-4f30-9237-045096e941be"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "475bbc34-11dd-48ab-bf4a-52a492057953",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "475bbc34-11dd-48ab-bf4a-52a492057953",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6a4637f0-da4b-4d60-92a9-c1fef49d0ac2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5cbe2525-ea77-438a-b917-12bad504cf27",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the Question of the Day, then click the animated slide to display the two examples on the slide, asking students to raise their hand if they've felt that problem themselves. Invite one or two volunteers to share an app idea of their own. Keep this quick and low-pressure — the goal is a fun, casual close to the period, not a deep discussion."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5cbe2525-ea77-438a-b917-12bad504cf27",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6a4637f0-da4b-4d60-92a9-c1fef49d0ac2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ed7a2bf2-e044-40ea-8b8a-7ea83feccf21",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Have students talk through the questions on the screen with a partner.\n- *What are apps?*\n- *How do we interact with them?*\n- *What kinds of things do apps do?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that apps solve problems or do everyday tasks, such as entertainment (images, movies, TV), social (chatting, social media), commerce (marketplaces), and finding information (search engines, maps). Students should also recognize that apps have screens, or user interfaces, that collect information from users with buttons, sliders, and text boxes, and display information back with sounds, images, video, and text."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ed7a2bf2-e044-40ea-8b8a-7ea83feccf21",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bd88188d-1c0b-41d3-84e1-ea44064b8587"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "209cd164-873b-47c3-ab44-8b3b49a654d0",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "209cd164-873b-47c3-ab44-8b3b49a654d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bd88188d-1c0b-41d3-84e1-ea44064b8587"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "26b5a18d-1d59-416b-9333-c86dfa3c5a16",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "26b5a18d-1d59-416b-9333-c86dfa3c5a16",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bd88188d-1c0b-41d3-84e1-ea44064b8587"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df1073b7-5454-4f0b-a404-a97fabe3f7f4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students try out sample apps and describe how users interact with each app, what its purpose is, and who it is for.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "App Exploration"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df1073b7-5454-4f0b-a404-a97fabe3f7f4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "335e6312-817b-4b6d-b723-39f381478601"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "698272a5-a956-4017-baec-3384394d0748",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we are going to look at some sample apps to explore their purpose and function.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "698272a5-a956-4017-baec-3384394d0748",
        "lesson_activity.key": "335e6312-817b-4b6d-b723-39f381478601"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0ff1a8cc-8433-45f9-8679-d1ad239c751d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students work in pairs. Depending on time, they explore at least three of the five apps."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0ff1a8cc-8433-45f9-8679-d1ad239c751d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "335e6312-817b-4b6d-b723-39f381478601"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "22820190-156e-432e-af5a-2b304c971103",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to work in pairs through Levels 1 to 5.\n- **Level 1 Water Conservation Tips:** Users switch between English and Spanish, choose a refillable bottle and fill it, and start a shower timer to learn ways to save water.\n- **Level 2 Bird Quiz:** Users answer three bird trivia questions, moving on after a right answer and landing on a losing screen after a wrong one.\n- **Level 3 Hamilton Township:** Users change the color of the town's flower baskets and click pictures to learn how the town is beautifying its streets.\n- **Level 4 Four Square:** Users click through screens that explain how to play and answer how many players are needed.\n- **Level 5 Monarch Butterflies:** Users click each life stage to see a description and follow the monarch life cycle.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for students who describe what they did in the app, not just what the screen looks like.",
        "progression_name": "App Exploration"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "22820190-156e-432e-af5a-2b304c971103",
        "lesson_activity.key": "335e6312-817b-4b6d-b723-39f381478601"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dc2fc456-f107-4c70-bd84-95519b91cab1",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Choose one app and talk it through with your partner. Write your ideas on scratch paper first.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dc2fc456-f107-4c70-bd84-95519b91cab1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "335e6312-817b-4b6d-b723-39f381478601"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c80b5c8d-db5a-4c14-b534-942a013ddb5e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the discussion prompts:\n- *How does the user interact with the app?*\n- *What is the purpose of this app?*\n- *Who is the target audience?*"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c80b5c8d-db5a-4c14-b534-942a013ddb5e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "335e6312-817b-4b6d-b723-39f381478601"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "609c6413-413d-4c6f-bbc8-514d1f6fa261",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students learn the vocabulary for how apps take in and send out information.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Play the video “How Computers Work.” Have students listen for the words input and output.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Input and Output"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "609c6413-413d-4c6f-bbc8-514d1f6fa261",
        "lesson_activity.key": "335e6312-817b-4b6d-b723-39f381478601"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aea35d1b-59f6-4ed8-af86-53a0f533d5f3",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *input*. Explain that an app gets input in many ways, from a user or from another program."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aea35d1b-59f6-4ed8-af86-53a0f533d5f3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "335e6312-817b-4b6d-b723-39f381478601"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ccb25287-4862-4299-8625-adee0776eeb0",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *output*. Explain that an app's output usually depends on its input, or on how the program was set up. For example, you could program an app to play a song as soon as it runs."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ccb25287-4862-4299-8625-adee0776eeb0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "335e6312-817b-4b6d-b723-39f381478601"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f2771bad-22b6-4d07-9835-f1d448be60f2",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *user interface*. Explain that the buttons, pictures, and text on an app's screen are its user interface."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f2771bad-22b6-4d07-9835-f1d448be60f2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "335e6312-817b-4b6d-b723-39f381478601"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4d707f7b-9443-4365-9902-5aaab6fafc6e",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students identify the inputs and outputs of the sample apps by trying actions and observing the results.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to work in pairs through Levels 6 to 10.\n- **Level 6 Water Conservation Tips:** Clicking Spanish changes the text, clicking the refillable bottle and then the faucet fills the bottle, and clicking the timer ends the countdown.\n- **Level 7 Bird Quiz:** Clicking an answer moves to the next question or to the losing screen, and three right answers lead to the winning screen.\n- **Level 8 Hamilton Township:** Clicking a color button changes the color box, and clicking a picture shows where that item belongs.\n- **Level 9 Four Square:** Clicking a number shows \"Not enough\" or \"Right!\", and clicking More Info moves to the next screen.\n- **Level 10 Monarch Butterflies:** Clicking the caterpillar or chrysalis shows its description, and clicking the butterfly reveals the Home button.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students pair every input with the output it caused.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "App Investigation",
        "progression_name": "App Investigation"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4d707f7b-9443-4365-9902-5aaab6fafc6e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "335e6312-817b-4b6d-b723-39f381478601"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "172f8e73-0804-4eda-aa65-09bc4df462b7",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What input did you try, and what output did it cause?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that inputs are actions like clicking a button, and outputs are what the app shows or plays in response."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "172f8e73-0804-4eda-aa65-09bc4df462b7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "335e6312-817b-4b6d-b723-39f381478601"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4142eae2-5b4c-45ca-9e3e-db3c595de1ed",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Check whether students can match input, output, and user interface to examples, state an app's purpose, and explain how they learned what an app does by using it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Level 11, the vocabulary match, and Level 12, the written reflection.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use these levels to check whether students can match an input, an output, and a user interface to examples, state an app's purpose, and describe an action they tried and how the app responded."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4142eae2-5b4c-45ca-9e3e-db3c595de1ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "335e6312-817b-4b6d-b723-39f381478601"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b7f9f61-4e3d-4918-b3a4-e0547af19844",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b7f9f61-4e3d-4918-b3a4-e0547af19844",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f406c1c-9e7e-4b03-b21f-4fe1f6968c54"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c885c334-f8e9-4b00-bb86-84645fb27447",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c885c334-f8e9-4b00-bb86-84645fb27447",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f406c1c-9e7e-4b03-b21f-4fe1f6968c54"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "633ad166-719d-44c1-bcce-8af8461c4e9c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Apps have different purposes for different users. They take in input and send out output, and users interact with them through the user interface.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "633ad166-719d-44c1-bcce-8af8461c4e9c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f406c1c-9e7e-4b03-b21f-4fe1f6968c54"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7dfb817a-41b1-47e5-b164-23225dfa450e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How could you tell what an app does just by using it?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that using an app and watching how it responds shows its purpose and behavior."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7dfb817a-41b1-47e5-b164-23225dfa450e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f406c1c-9e7e-4b03-b21f-4fe1f6968c54"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8fb28cce-13b3-426a-a486-6ce017fc7520",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8fb28cce-13b3-426a-a486-6ce017fc7520",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f406c1c-9e7e-4b03-b21f-4fe1f6968c54"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "374c1639-aee7-4c45-89da-bc9ddc21f6e0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Get students thinking about how much their devices notice on their own, before the terms \"automatic input\" and \"user input\" are introduced.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** List as many ways as you can that a phone, laptop, or watch collects information on its own to work and to run apps.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students realize their devices collect far more information on their own than they expected. Record their answers on the board and keep them. You'll point back to this list on the \"What is an automatic input?\" slide. Listen for: location, time, light level (auto-brightness), motion (screen rotation, step count), face unlock, battery level, Wi-Fi and Bluetooth, and \"Hey Siri\" listening.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "374c1639-aee7-4c45-89da-bc9ddc21f6e0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a21b15bf-8982-4fd0-9e84-eb6a7e3d695c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ae4db31e-df8d-42a9-83b2-f3b17cf9b7bb",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ae4db31e-df8d-42a9-83b2-f3b17cf9b7bb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a21b15bf-8982-4fd0-9e84-eb6a7e3d695c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8adb6339-da00-4c34-a59b-25af446564e2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students learn the difference between user input and automatic input, then test four simulated sensors to see how inputs change an app's output.\n\n\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Inputs and Test a Sensor"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8adb6339-da00-4c34-a59b-25af446564e2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0b407ac-df1b-4685-90bb-09f1a3efd1ea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0b20dbe4-acdc-40dc-b06e-4c37a70efb07",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Have students work in pairs in Lesson 3, Level 1: Test a Sensor. Have students try every sensor and watch what happens on the phone.\" The level has a Sensor Panel on the left and a phone on the right. Each sensor changes something different on the phone. Encourage pairs to try each sensor more than once and to change one thing at a time so they can see exactly what it affects.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ask pairs:\n* \"What happens at 0 and 100 on the Light Sensor?\"\n* \"Did changing the city change anything you didn't expect?\"\n* \"What happened when someone else picked up the phone?\"\n",
        "progression_name": "Test a Sensor",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The microphone reads replies out loud. Have students use headphones, or turn off \"Read replies out loud\" so the room doesn't get noisy.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0b20dbe4-acdc-40dc-b06e-4c37a70efb07",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0b407ac-df1b-4685-90bb-09f1a3efd1ea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3f0744bc-801d-4284-b5d7-fac4709e0a05",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Which does a phone use on its own? Which need your permission or action first? Which are both?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students see that most automatic inputs only work because the user gave permission or took action first.\n* Light Sensor (on its own): Adjusts the screen without asking.\n* GPS (both): You tap \"Allow\" once, then it tracks you on its own.\n* Front Camera (both): You set up your face, then it checks on its own.\n* Microphone (both): You give permission, then it listens for \"Hey Siri.\"\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3f0744bc-801d-4284-b5d7-fac4709e0a05",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0b407ac-df1b-4685-90bb-09f1a3efd1ea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9ceed25f-1f3a-4ad6-ab8e-9de2e3fd9865",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define user input. Ask students for one example that isn't on the slide, such as taking a photo, scrolling, or filling out a form.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define automatic input. Point back to the warm-up list on the board and say, \"Most of what you listed are automatic inputs. Your phone collected them without you doing anything.\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Make it personal. If permitted, have students open their phone's location settings (on iPhone: Settings → Privacy & Security → Location Services; on Android: Settings → Location → App permissions) and count how many apps have location access. Most students are surprised by the number and have forgotten saying yes to most of them. This sets up the \"permission\" discussion after Test a Sensor.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9ceed25f-1f3a-4ad6-ab8e-9de2e3fd9865",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0b407ac-df1b-4685-90bb-09f1a3efd1ea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1531c41a-0a52-4110-b3b7-0ab9d4ed3482",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1531c41a-0a52-4110-b3b7-0ab9d4ed3482",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0b407ac-df1b-4685-90bb-09f1a3efd1ea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e9fbd894-c8c3-4ec5-a2b9-2f20097bf012",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define hidden output. \n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e9fbd894-c8c3-4ec5-a2b9-2f20097bf012",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0b407ac-df1b-4685-90bb-09f1a3efd1ea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "257174eb-450e-46ac-94cd-30fce3389a3f",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "257174eb-450e-46ac-94cd-30fce3389a3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0b407ac-df1b-4685-90bb-09f1a3efd1ea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "26f2751c-4a26-40b3-81ef-6c6d1696ee95",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students learn to trace how information moves through an app using the ISPO model. Then they apply it to a real app scenario, including at least one hidden output.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through each stage in order, pointing to each card as you go:\n* **Input:** \"This is what the app takes in, either from you or automatically.\"\n* **Storage:** \"This is what the app saves, like your settings or history.\"\n* **Process:** \"This is where the app turns that information into something useful.\"\n* **Output:** \"This is what the app produces. Some of it you see, and some of it you never see.\"\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "The ISPO Model"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "26f2751c-4a26-40b3-81ef-6c6d1696ee95",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0b407ac-df1b-4685-90bb-09f1a3efd1ea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "973d591e-925c-476b-94fa-d2359e141051",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click to reveal each stage one at a time, then click to bring in the phone. Pause on two points: Input: The user never said where they were. Location was an automatic input; Output: Point to the hidden output. Ask, \"Did you know your voice recording leaves your phone?\"\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students asked this same question in Test a Sensor. Remind them that when they pressed Speak, the same four stages happened behind the scenes.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "973d591e-925c-476b-94fa-d2359e141051",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0b407ac-df1b-4685-90bb-09f1a3efd1ea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4d8460a7-d3a9-4913-8a1e-189c63a1a19d",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Have students work in pairs in Lesson 3, Level 2: Trace Input to Output. Assign scenarios A-D so all four are covered. Sending a text (B) is the most familiar, and scanning a QR code (D) is the biggest challenge. In the level, students sort pre-written cards into the Input, Storage, Process, and Output zones, then fill in blank cards with their own ideas. Every flowchart must include an automatic input, a visible output, and a hidden output.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ask pairs:\n* Stuck on Storage: \"When you open this app tomorrow, what does it still remember?\"\n* Everything in Output: \"Which of these would the user actually see?\"\n* No hidden output: \"Did any information leave the phone? Where did it go?\"\n",
        "progression_name": "Trace Input to Output",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use the completed Level 2 flowcharts as a quick formative check. Did students sort the cards into the right stages? Look closely at two cards: the automatic input, such as \"GPS finds where you are,\" and the hidden output, such as \"Your location is sent to the map company.\" Placing these correctly shows students can tell automatic input from user input and hidden output from visible output. This can help you gauge how well the class understands the first two objectives before moving on to edge cases.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4d8460a7-d3a9-4913-8a1e-189c63a1a19d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0b407ac-df1b-4685-90bb-09f1a3efd1ea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "52e68b7f-1041-4a7c-a26f-defeda7fd38d",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students explain how an app should respond when conditions change, and what feedback the user should get. This builds their understanding of why apps need to behave as intended across a range of conditions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a copy of the Handle an Edge Case activity guide.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Handle an Edge Case"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "52e68b7f-1041-4a7c-a26f-defeda7fd38d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0b407ac-df1b-4685-90bb-09f1a3efd1ea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b058cdbb-06f3-45c9-8a75-4b2003e2f1c7",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Define edge case."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b058cdbb-06f3-45c9-8a75-4b2003e2f1c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0b407ac-df1b-4685-90bb-09f1a3efd1ea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "472e2fd4-6c2b-41d4-82c3-09ed50eddfc8",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Have students go back to Level 2 to find the edge case for the scenario they traced. They look back at their flowchart, check which stages would change, and write how the app should respond and what the user should see or hear.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use the completed activity guides as a quick formative check. Did students name a specific app response, such as \"saves the message and tries again,\" rather than \"it shows an error\"? Does their feedback tell the user what happened and what to do next? This helps you gauge the third objective: why apps need to behave as intended across a range of conditions.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "472e2fd4-6c2b-41d4-82c3-09ed50eddfc8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0b407ac-df1b-4685-90bb-09f1a3efd1ea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f65f2104-4cda-4246-abaf-4de6375aa3b9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f65f2104-4cda-4246-abaf-4de6375aa3b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f21c65e3-fad0-4a93-99d4-ada80f072257"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "66fdbe5d-4075-491d-b979-bbe0a814fb5e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "66fdbe5d-4075-491d-b979-bbe0a814fb5e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f21c65e3-fad0-4a93-99d4-ada80f072257"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ecfdcb8a-3342-4aaf-93b3-21b7b07ef89b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:**  Students share their own ideas about what information is and begin to see that computer science is about information, not just computers. This sets up the working definition used in the rest of the lesson: information is the answer to a question.\n\n\n\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ecfdcb8a-3342-4aaf-93b3-21b7b07ef89b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3fe14184-f0a2-4aa9-9692-d295cb54d5a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0b814d32-6a8c-4a38-b019-7b447f084bb8",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A lot of people think that computer science is the study of computers, like the phone in your pocket or the computer on your desk. As we'll see this year, computer science actually has a lot more to do with information.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0b814d32-6a8c-4a38-b019-7b447f084bb8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3fe14184-f0a2-4aa9-9692-d295cb54d5a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "de47f96c-6d91-441a-967b-7c304e8f1d23",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What is your definition of information?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:**  More important than writing a formal definition of information is getting students discussing the term and its diverse forms. If students struggle with defining \"information,\" modify the prompt by asking them to provide examples of information in their world such as texts, books, videos, music, conversations, etc."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "de47f96c-6d91-441a-967b-7c304e8f1d23",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3fe14184-f0a2-4aa9-9692-d295cb54d5a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7855cb36-4c41-444e-9156-05e5ac4f5bdb",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7855cb36-4c41-444e-9156-05e5ac4f5bdb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3fe14184-f0a2-4aa9-9692-d295cb54d5a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aa69b488-d6d9-4191-90dc-01c692a3aea5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students build and revise their own devices to send 2, then 4, then 8 possible messages. Along the way, they discover that reusing a small set of options in sequence scales better than inventing a new option for every message. This lays the groundwork for representing information with bits.\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Information Sending Devices"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aa69b488-d6d9-4191-90dc-01c692a3aea5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b2ac46c7-c309-4c94-bf71-edc6a1239188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cc17c089-bd6b-4853-9abd-12775c99577f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today you are going to build your own device for sending and receiving messages which you will use to answer questions that you write.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cc17c089-bd6b-4853-9abd-12775c99577f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b2ac46c7-c309-4c94-bf71-edc6a1239188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a334b77c-5a1d-4136-b36b-76caf15a2741",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Materials for students to build their binary message devices (straws, scissors, etc.).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in pairs.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a334b77c-5a1d-4136-b36b-76caf15a2741",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b2ac46c7-c309-4c94-bf71-edc6a1239188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c8a2515-9018-43f7-8f07-7f7ae765e9f3",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to write down a question that has two possible answers.",
        "name": "Challenge #1",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "In this activity, avoid the urge to give students a pre-written question or answers without allowing them time to struggle with the challenge. Encourage students to write down how their device works.\n* Example Question: Do you like strawberry or vanilla ice cream?\n* Answer: strawberry (move my pencil up and down), vanilla (move my pencil side to side)\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c8a2515-9018-43f7-8f07-7f7ae765e9f3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b2ac46c7-c309-4c94-bf71-edc6a1239188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e57714e9-927a-4c82-887d-9fbcaf5d3315",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to build a device out of classroom supplies to communicate the answer to their question. Remind students of the rules:\n* No projectiles.\n* No language can be used. For example: If my question is \"Is your favorite color blue or green?\" I can't write the words blue and green on my device.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e57714e9-927a-4c82-887d-9fbcaf5d3315",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b2ac46c7-c309-4c94-bf71-edc6a1239188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "76ba725a-331e-40ef-8ed5-41b210af60f9",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** After students have completed their devices, choose a few groups to demonstrate their device in action. Students stand on opposite sides of the classroom. One student asks the agreed upon question. The other student uses the device to communicate the answer."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "76ba725a-331e-40ef-8ed5-41b210af60f9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b2ac46c7-c309-4c94-bf71-edc6a1239188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b13793ed-9641-417b-9c88-3989a3e69a86",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to modify the answer to their question so there are now four possible answers.",
        "name": "Challenge #2",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students may add new ways of answering a question. Others may notice that they can reuse their previous responses, sending those responses in sequences that produce new messages. Avoid the urge to tell them to reuse the device, instead allowing them to explore their own ideas.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b13793ed-9641-417b-9c88-3989a3e69a86",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b2ac46c7-c309-4c94-bf71-edc6a1239188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c2ca4715-4700-4155-aa2f-c8a0f0e9c78b",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to update their device to communicate one of four possible answers to their question.  Encourage them to consider:\n* Should you modify your device?\n* Can you use it in a different way?\n* Should you make a new device?\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c2ca4715-4700-4155-aa2f-c8a0f0e9c78b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b2ac46c7-c309-4c94-bf71-edc6a1239188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e43e3db6-f72f-40ef-97bb-af96d7f3386f",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Again, choose groups to demonstrate their updated devices.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e43e3db6-f72f-40ef-97bb-af96d7f3386f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b2ac46c7-c309-4c94-bf71-edc6a1239188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "44291c50-87f7-40c8-b0af-ac1f75dbaef6",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to modify the answers to their question so there are now eight possible choices.",
        "name": "Challenge #3",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "At this point, students may start to realize that the way their device communicates information is not practical and could not be scaled up if there were for example, a thousand or a million possible answers.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "44291c50-87f7-40c8-b0af-ac1f75dbaef6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b2ac46c7-c309-4c94-bf71-edc6a1239188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "326f70e1-45ac-4e5e-82ef-4eb3948fd7ff",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to update their device to communicate one of eight possible answers to your question. Encourage them to consider:\n* Should you modify your device?\n* Can you use it in a different way?\n* Should you make a new device?\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "326f70e1-45ac-4e5e-82ef-4eb3948fd7ff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b2ac46c7-c309-4c94-bf71-edc6a1239188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b99f979d-bcef-4c02-9069-d336c0af7d1d",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Do a final demonstration of devices before ending the activity.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b99f979d-bcef-4c02-9069-d336c0af7d1d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b2ac46c7-c309-4c94-bf71-edc6a1239188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "39af2b67-fa6d-433f-b55c-843c922d4925",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 1 to answer the checking for understanding question.\n",
        "progression_name": "Checking for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this level to check whether students can explain that a sequence of two options can represent many different messages, describe why reusing the same two options scales better than adding a new option for every message, and give an example of combining two options to send more than two messages.\n\nIf many students still describe adding new options for each message, run a short reflection discussion: revisit a pair's Challenge #1 device and have the class figure out together how to send four messages using only its two original options.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "39af2b67-fa6d-433f-b55c-843c922d4925",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b2ac46c7-c309-4c94-bf71-edc6a1239188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cc7c1979-aa59-467b-9324-24eb8545aef2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let's wrap up by discussing how you designed your devices and what that means about the questions you can ask and the messages you can send.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cc7c1979-aa59-467b-9324-24eb8545aef2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3cf9487-6de8-4b7e-8e3c-ab6d29b596bd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7138b11-3d76-48dd-8d9a-30474f8cd631",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Think back to your simple two-option device from Challenge #1. Instead of changing your device and adding more options every time you added more answers, how could you simply modify the way you use your device with only two options?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Focus the discussion on why some groups created devices using unique options for each additional message, while other groups devised plans in which they reused the same device to create new combinations of the original two options.\n\nUltimately, groups begin to notice that, for example: instead of bending a straw in four different directions, they could simply bend the straw the same two ways multiple times.\n* Sample Question: Do you like strawberry, chocolate, vanilla, or peanut butter ice cream?\n* Answers: strawberry (bend straw forward once), chocolate (bend straw forward twice), vanilla (bend straw backwards once), peanut butter (bend straw backwards twice)\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7138b11-3d76-48dd-8d9a-30474f8cd631",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3cf9487-6de8-4b7e-8e3c-ab6d29b596bd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "019590d6-5c06-4c8b-b250-f700d853baa2",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today. Have students share with a neighbor before having a quick class discussion.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "019590d6-5c06-4c8b-b250-f700d853baa2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3cf9487-6de8-4b7e-8e3c-ab6d29b596bd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9745896a-b968-4d4b-9a2b-0c1db7d8f229",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Surface that a number can be represented many different ways (words, symbols, pictures, math expressions) so students are primed to accept that circles and squares are just another valid way to represent information.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9745896a-b968-4d4b-9a2b-0c1db7d8f229",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bff51886-8d19-4eee-bb71-15a4503074a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bef5dde7-edb4-40a1-a13f-19a1bac3f526",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "There are a variety of ways we could represent the number 7 - we might use the numeral '7' or the word 'seven', but this might be different in other countries or other languages. Today we'll see how we might represent the number 7 using only two different shapes.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bef5dde7-edb4-40a1-a13f-19a1bac3f526",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bff51886-8d19-4eee-bb71-15a4503074a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4fde4d9f-e492-40e6-805b-0f4a2a9fc7d8",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How many ways can you represent 7?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Introduce the idea that the numeral '7' is just one commonly used symbol to represent the number seven. There are many ways to communicate this same number that may use different symbols or representations, all of which are valid. Sharing the variety of responses helps motivate the following activity that asks students to discover a system for using the circles and squares to represent numbers, including the number 7.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students should brainstorm individually before sharing in small groups. It is important to allow ample brainstorming time - students may generate familiar responses at first, but may stretch their thinking and get more creative with additional time. Some examples may include:\n* Linguistic examples - \"seven\", \"siete\" (spanish), \"sept\" (french), \"sieben\" (german), etc\n* Picture examples - dots, tallys, emojis, etc\n* Math & Geometry examples - 5 + 2, 8 - 1, a seven-sided shape, etc\n\nAfter a short time, ask students to share some responses with the whole class. Use these responses to quickly generate a wide variety of representations.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4fde4d9f-e492-40e6-805b-0f4a2a9fc7d8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bff51886-8d19-4eee-bb71-15a4503074a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f8f5ec19-5735-4fae-9ea3-428ed8ff6311",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f8f5ec19-5735-4fae-9ea3-428ed8ff6311",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bff51886-8d19-4eee-bb71-15a4503074a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b363f60d-cc01-419f-b23b-dc72785072d2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Get students producing a raw, unordered list of possible patterns first, so the ordering problem (everyone got a different 7th pattern) becomes visible and motivates the need for rules.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Generate Patterns"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b363f60d-cc01-419f-b23b-dc72785072d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f40cf622-c6d7-43c0-8b22-29c6f4640a02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b4f9037b-e285-4002-842f-67bb74b21d45",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the previous class, we ended by deciding that one of the best way to use our devices was to limit them to two options, let's say: option 1 is circles and option 2 is squares. Now let's figure out how we can use these shapes to communicate lots of different pieces of information.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b4f9037b-e285-4002-842f-67bb74b21d45",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f40cf622-c6d7-43c0-8b22-29c6f4640a02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e052d77c-e8b0-459f-a604-39e2ad07cfa8",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Group students in pairs and direct them to work out how many different pieces of information (made of up of circles and squares) they can represent with three place values. \n* For example: circle-circle-circle and circle-square-circle can represent two different pieces of information.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e052d77c-e8b0-459f-a604-39e2ad07cfa8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f40cf622-c6d7-43c0-8b22-29c6f4640a02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6978930c-a3a3-454b-90d1-7a32a857f146",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We agreed that there are 8 possible patterns we can make with 3 place values. But, not everyone wrote these patterns in the same order, which means we don't all have the same 7th pattern! Our goal is to create a clear set of rules where, if the class were to follow these rules, everyone should generate the same list of patterns in the same order.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6978930c-a3a3-454b-90d1-7a32a857f146",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f40cf622-c6d7-43c0-8b22-29c6f4640a02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9d3ca7b2-6ddf-44b7-95c3-23116a148cf9",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Share out your 7th pattern\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:**  Confirm with the class that there are 8 possible patterns, but don't list all of them out. Ask students to share the 7th pattern in their list. Students will likely have different answers for this.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9d3ca7b2-6ddf-44b7-95c3-23116a148cf9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f40cf622-c6d7-43c0-8b22-29c6f4640a02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f37cdc62-a07a-4ac7-875f-794f50e08c30",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:**  Have groups create their own clear, reproducible rules for ordering patterns, then test those rules by extending them to a harder case (four place values) that the original rules may not fully cover.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in groups of 2, making one group of 3 if necessary\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a copy of the Circle Square Patterns handout. Each group also gets the Shape Cutouts to use during the activity.\n",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Circle Square Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Manipulatives: Students are given manipulatives to help visualize any rules they are using to move from one element of the list to the next. You might see students use all their manipulatives at once to create the different patterns, then discuss how to arrange them into an ordered list. You might see students representing one pattern at a time, then discussing the rules for “replacing” shapes to generate each of the next patterns. Students might not use the manipulatives at all, using pen & paper or whiteboards instead.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f37cdc62-a07a-4ac7-875f-794f50e08c30",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f40cf622-c6d7-43c0-8b22-29c6f4640a02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "37b5a262-2302-478a-ae88-b0cf3af9d8dd",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to again list all possible three place value patterns, but with an added focus on the order of their patterns.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "* **Many Possible Answers:** It is okay for different groups to come up with different orders for their lists of patterns - this will help with the share-out discussion as you highlight different strategies.\n* **Facilitating With Groups:** You should act as a facilitator during this part of the activity, guiding students in describing the rules & strategies that they used to create their list. These strategies may be implicit and unconscious to the student, but you can ask questions to help students realize their own thinking that went into generating their list. Aim to help students clarify their thinking to make it easier for other groups to follow.\n* **Group Dynamics:** Be mindful of groups that appear to be dominated by a single student. Asking each student individually about their strategy can help bring students back together and reinforce the collaborative aspect of this activity.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "37b5a262-2302-478a-ae88-b0cf3af9d8dd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f40cf622-c6d7-43c0-8b22-29c6f4640a02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "41af2276-b1bf-4d81-b972-4b538839b06d",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to describe the rules or strategy they used to create their list. They are aiming for clear directions that can be followed by another group to reproduce their same list in the same order."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "41af2276-b1bf-4d81-b972-4b538839b06d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f40cf622-c6d7-43c0-8b22-29c6f4640a02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "56343f73-3bb3-4cec-9cc8-c494e89fdcf6",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to extend their rules to generate all possible four place value patterns. This challenge has 2 parts: discovering all possible four place value combinations (there are 16) and extending the rules from the previous challenge so they work here as well. Most groups will need to change or add to their rules in order to accomplish Challenge #3."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "56343f73-3bb3-4cec-9cc8-c494e89fdcf6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f40cf622-c6d7-43c0-8b22-29c6f4640a02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3084c7e9-9745-4df3-9a31-db9d392d9b3f",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Share your rules with the class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface that multiple different rule sets can all be valid, as long as they're clear enough for another group to follow and reproduce the same list. There's no single correct set of rules, only clearer or less clear ones.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "There are many ways to structure this discussion, especially if you have your own established share-out routines. Here are a few that could work for this particular discussion:\n* Have each group trade with another group, and each group tries to re-create the original group's list. This strategy is useful if you have more class time than expected during the wrap-up.\n* A group reads their rules while you and the rest of the class try to recreate the list\n* A group reveals their list and the class tries to predict what the rules are, then the group shares their rules with the class\n* You can name different strategies you’ve seen from groups as you’ve been circulating, then ask groups to give a thumbs-up if their rules involved that particular pattern. This strategy is useful if you’re running short on time before the next part of the lesson.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3084c7e9-9745-4df3-9a31-db9d392d9b3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f40cf622-c6d7-43c0-8b22-29c6f4640a02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "73255842-cacd-40ee-84e4-526f195a29fa",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 1 to complete the Checking for Understanding question.",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 1 can be used as a formative assessment to check student understanding of the lesson objectives.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "73255842-cacd-40ee-84e4-526f195a29fa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f40cf622-c6d7-43c0-8b22-29c6f4640a02"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "589af74a-1282-4028-89ab-2691cad49f98",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Congratulations! You just invented your own system for counting and representing information using only two symbols. Here's the interesting part: your circles and squares could have meant anything, as long as your group agreed on the rules. Tomorrow we'll look at the two-symbol system computers actually use, and see how the same patterns can represent totally different things depending on what we agree they mean.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "589af74a-1282-4028-89ab-2691cad49f98",
        "lesson_activity.key": "37da8147-ea66-48fa-aa23-1bdcb4f21727"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "345c3b0a-b248-4d5f-bb70-40b68e50f9ae",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today. \n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "345c3b0a-b248-4d5f-bb70-40b68e50f9ae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "37da8147-ea66-48fa-aa23-1bdcb4f21727"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e712fdda-468b-4dc3-ac98-a7d04b963d06",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n* *How is counting in this circle and square system similar to how we count in our regular lives?*  \n* *How is it different?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Similar: It follows agreed-upon rules that everyone agrees to follow. Different: Uses shapes instead of numbers, and the same shapes could represent something else entirely if we'd agreed on different rules.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e712fdda-468b-4dc3-ac98-a7d04b963d06",
        "lesson_activity.key": "37da8147-ea66-48fa-aa23-1bdcb4f21727"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7bdd5c30-cad1-4fd1-9400-fffd2d05a785",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Activate what students already know about representing information with only two options, and connect that prior experience to the binary number system they'll build today.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *Last class you created your own number system using circles and squares. What can we communicate using only two symbols?*\n- *Is there a limit?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** In previous lessons, students represented information using two options. This is a quick-thinking question to tap into students’ prior knowledge and experiences. Once students have mentioned a few of the points below, they can move on.\n- The answer to a yes/no or true/false question\n- Flipping a switch on/off\n- Combinations of yes/no answers by using multiple symbols in a row\n- We can keep adding more of the same symbols, so the only limit is how much space we have to write or store those symbols",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use the Retrieve-Pair-Share strategy to facilitate this discussion by directing students to write down their thoughts and ideas first, then pair up students to compare their answers and expand their notes. This allows time for individual processing and idea generation before sharing thoughts with others. Ask students to share the ideas they discussed with their partners as a class.\n\nFor more ideas on how to adapt this strategy to fit the needs of your class, check out the CSP Guide to Classroom Discussions"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7bdd5c30-cad1-4fd1-9400-fffd2d05a785",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e3f9759b-edbe-468f-ba18-4206de91c4d2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c8365cad-d0e1-4e79-9aba-a90acf9affdc",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c8365cad-d0e1-4e79-9aba-a90acf9affdc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e3f9759b-edbe-468f-ba18-4206de91c4d2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ca4344d5-3613-447d-861a-602b0de0f098",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ca4344d5-3613-447d-861a-602b0de0f098",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e3f9759b-edbe-468f-ba18-4206de91c4d2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff7ec340-7678-4499-b759-5da38d41fce0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Build an understanding of how binary (base 2) works by connecting it to the circle-square patterns from the previous lesson, then use the Flippy Do to convert between decimal and binary and see how adding bits increases the number of values that can be represented.",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Binary Numbers"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff7ec340-7678-4499-b759-5da38d41fce0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f9d668fe-1c9c-4d00-b953-c2de53d6a5a5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we are going to explore how Binary Numbers work.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f9d668fe-1c9c-4d00-b953-c2de53d6a5a5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f673471-504c-4be0-8321-189d6e6cadda",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Instruct students to take out their copy of the CSP Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f673471-504c-4be0-8321-189d6e6cadda",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8c7d16e7-ac03-4ad4-906e-79bddb5e816f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "With only one place value, we only have two possible patterns: circle or square.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8c7d16e7-ac03-4ad4-906e-79bddb5e816f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "766889a8-7b00-45c9-b5ad-3521c81110cd",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to display the patterns. Explain that we  started with circle, but we could have easily started with square instead."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "766889a8-7b00-45c9-b5ad-3521c81110cd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b4a5c848-d17e-44b7-89aa-ea1e65158c90",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to display the patterns. Explain that with two place values, we can make two sets of the previous patterns. Then, we put circles in front of the first set and squares in front of the second set and this makes four possible patterns."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b4a5c848-d17e-44b7-89aa-ea1e65158c90",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "052f324c-4aa9-4923-8776-126fcd812aa9",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to display the patterns. Explain that for three place value patterns, we can group the two place value patterns together, make a copy, and then, just like we did before, put circles in front of the first set and squares in front of the second set. This makes 8 arrangements. Note that computer scientists like to start counting at 0!"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "052f324c-4aa9-4923-8776-126fcd812aa9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dfd48d27-ad05-4072-b784-eb1ccf4a639c",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Instead of two shapes, what if we had 10 shapes?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dfd48d27-ad05-4072-b784-eb1ccf4a639c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ca78c194-9d49-44b0-b0a7-997ba2adf36c",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to display the patterns. Explain that we could use more geometric shapes, or we could use letters, but the \"shapes\" we are used to are the numbers 0 through 9, but at the end of the day, they are really just \"shapes\"."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ca78c194-9d49-44b0-b0a7-997ba2adf36c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "270c3ee9-1bf4-4421-8353-efbfd362558c",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "With two places, we have one hundred 2-shape patterns. These are the numbers 00 through 99.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "270c3ee9-1bf4-4421-8353-efbfd362558c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e9e4d4e-ae53-4877-b451-959e507a26e5",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to display the patterns. Explain that just like the circles and squares, we can group them together, make copies, and then put the first shape, a 0, in front of the first set, put the next shape, a 1, in front of the next set, and so on until we have all 100 2-shape patterns."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e9e4d4e-ae53-4877-b451-959e507a26e5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "10ebe87e-e978-4af4-937f-53776663484a",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "What happens when we count up to and reach the last shape of our 2-shape pattern, 99?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "10ebe87e-e978-4af4-937f-53776663484a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d7e77b4f-4b5f-4636-a61f-a47cfcb8fe30",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students what comes after 99."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d7e77b4f-4b5f-4636-a61f-a47cfcb8fe30",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ec17cbf2-fde1-489d-a30b-d60fa360c5ba",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "100! When we get to the last shape, 9, we go back to the top and start over with the first shape, which is 0, and the next place to the left rolls to the next shape, 1.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ec17cbf2-fde1-489d-a30b-d60fa360c5ba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5c8a2dd2-8509-4cdf-a7b0-54e9fb46980b",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain to students how this is the mechanics behind place values that we have used all our lives. Make the connection to an \"old school\" odometer on a car where the numbers roll to the next number."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5c8a2dd2-8509-4cdf-a7b0-54e9fb46980b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "644f0073-3c1b-490b-b05e-9522a2302814",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Where is this heading?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "644f0073-3c1b-490b-b05e-9522a2302814",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb4c2a22-deb9-458a-8a47-ebfb9ff04fd5",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to introduce binary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb4c2a22-deb9-458a-8a47-ebfb9ff04fd5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c705da42-63a3-470d-8f58-c9c1f0217650",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Binary is a number system with two shapes. But those two shapes aren't a circle and square … or are they?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c705da42-63a3-470d-8f58-c9c1f0217650",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9dce4b3c-41f1-4d39-9d40-45612cd30ad0",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to connect the binary number system to the circle square activity from the previous lesson. Reveal that the two numbers used in binary are 0 and 1."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9dce4b3c-41f1-4d39-9d40-45612cd30ad0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "66ae06ae-a7f4-4485-83fb-bc046ac67ae7",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *decimal number*. Direct students to fill the blank in their unit guide for this vocab word."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "66ae06ae-a7f4-4485-83fb-bc046ac67ae7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6cd95330-1722-4c26-968c-82443bb8f83b",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *binary number*. Direct students to fill the blank in their unit guide for this vocab word."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6cd95330-1722-4c26-968c-82443bb8f83b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2273e51d-8429-4165-8e94-6b528e312648",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain that instead of shapes, we use 0's and 1's. In this example, each pattern maps to a decimal number from 0 to 7."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2273e51d-8429-4165-8e94-6b528e312648",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "90eed2ea-8b75-4f5e-95c7-4c508e411898",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a Flippy Do handout and Activity Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "90eed2ea-8b75-4f5e-95c7-4c508e411898",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a560811-a1f5-432b-8535-6f58c5fc6246",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "description": "For today's activity, you will be creating your own Flippy Do. This is a tool that will allow you to quickly and easily translate between the decimal number base we are used to as humans and the binary number base that computers use.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a560811-a1f5-432b-8535-6f58c5fc6246",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d263cc20-e843-4d8b-ac28-a52b5c5db1eb",
      "position": 25,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Lead students through completing their Flippy Do's using the slide as a guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d263cc20-e843-4d8b-ac28-a52b5c5db1eb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c2fed3b0-615f-45f9-874f-e0f66bb599f9",
      "position": 26,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Each place value represents one \"bit\" which is short for \"binary digit\". A binary digit can be a zero or a one. Your flippy do has eight \"bits\".",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c2fed3b0-615f-45f9-874f-e0f66bb599f9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "18a52d45-a39f-4e3d-ad27-0a804edf6e5a",
      "position": 27,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that together, eight place values, or \"bits\", makes up one \"byte\". Since computers represent information digitally, the lowest level components of information are bits."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "18a52d45-a39f-4e3d-ad27-0a804edf6e5a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "81d8bd53-43bf-4c2d-94a6-95cc0fc38700",
      "position": 28,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *bit*. Direct students to fill the blank in their unit guide for this vocab word."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "81d8bd53-43bf-4c2d-94a6-95cc0fc38700",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ef23fa9-46ec-4a47-828b-8847fc39f0b7",
      "position": 29,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *byte*. Direct students to fill the blank in their unit guide for this vocab word."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ef23fa9-46ec-4a47-828b-8847fc39f0b7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "03495094-1ce4-43a8-8fb1-202420cf60fe",
      "position": 30,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to use their Flippy Do to try out these six problems.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "It may be necessary to demonstrate how values can be calculated by flipping up a “1” for each value required to arrive at the sum of values equal to the decimal number.\n\nFor example, To convert the decimal number 10, I would flip up a one in the 8's position, because eight can fit in 10 (The next bit to the left is 16, which is too big). Then I have 2 left. I flip up a one in the 2's position. This gives me the binary number \"1010\", which means 10 in decimal.\n\nIf students are having a difficult time understanding the rules of the system, remind them of the concept of place value and relate to base 10."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "03495094-1ce4-43a8-8fb1-202420cf60fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dad73b83-c94c-4cb9-9f21-6f03dffb8ef7",
      "position": 31,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let's continue to practice with our own two number bases, decimal and binary. After you finish each of the four parts of the Activity Guide, I want you to check your work with your partner. Feel free to use your Flippy Do as you work.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dad73b83-c94c-4cb9-9f21-6f03dffb8ef7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "80107fb1-7d15-41fb-8d05-e79e549868fd",
      "position": 32,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Flippy Do activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As you circulate, take an opportunity to be a Lead Learner. Help students discover the items below using the suggested questions:\n- **Reading a number vs. Placing a Number:** Do we fill in the places on the Flippy Do starting on the left or right? Does it matter? (Yes. If we have a 5-bit number, we actually use the 5 bits on the far right. If we were to use the bits on the far left, this changes the value of the number. This is similar to adding more zeros to a decimal number.)\n- **Highest value possible with a given number of bits:** What is the largest number we can make with 4 bits? Is the last number we can make always odd? (A meaningful pattern is that we can count as high as one less than the next bit on the left. If we have four bits, we can count up to the number 15, because the next bit has a value of 16.)\n- **Number of numbers we can make**: How many total unique numbers are possible with 4 bits? (This is a base 2 number system. With each new bit, we double the amount of unique numbers we can make. With four bits, we can make the decimal numbers 0 to 15 (0000 to 1111), for a total of 16 unique numbers.)",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Challenge 1 - All 4-bit Numbers:** Students should produce all binary numbers with a length of 4 bits, from 0000 to 1111. They should see that all odd numbers end in 1 and even numbers end in 0. Students may also notice that the binary digits increasingly “roll-over” to 1’s (from right to left) as numbers become larger and larger.\n\n**Challenge 2 - Binary Numbers with Exactly One 1:** The goal here is for students to systematically find all binary numbers that have all zeros, except for one bit. Students should notice that the resulting decimal values are all powers of 2.\n\n**Challenge 3 - Conversion Practice:** This section gives students more practice converting between number bases. The last two decimal numbers, 256 and 513, are too big to represent using the Flippy Do, however students should make the connection that more bits could be added to the left of the Flippy Do using increasing powers of 2.\n\n**Challenge 4 - Putting it all Together:** The last four questions on the Activity Guide ask students to apply their understanding to new situations. The first two questions ask students to describe what happens to the value when 0’s are added to the left or right of a binary number. The last two questions ask students to think about how many bits are required to represent specific values in binary."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "80107fb1-7d15-41fb-8d05-e79e549868fd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b30f5394-e97d-4c95-adb5-9b76c53b24ea",
      "position": 33,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Check whether students can determine place value in a binary number, explain how the number of bits limits the values that can be represented, and compare the binary and decimal number systems.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 4.\n- **Level 1:** Students choose which answer best describes the effect of moving from a 4-bit to an 8-bit student ID system.\n- **Level 2:** Students reflect on what happens to the number of possible values each time a bit is added.\n- **Level 3:** Students reflect on how many bits would be needed to count everyone in class today.\n- **Level 4:** Students explain the similarities and differences between the binary and decimal number systems.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Levels 1 - 4 can be used as a formative assessment to check student understanding of the lesson objectives."
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Level 1 works well for Peer Instruction. After students answer individually, ask a few to share their reasoning, then have students discuss with a partner who chose a different answer before revealing the correct response."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b30f5394-e97d-4c95-adb5-9b76c53b24ea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0039aed3-b32f-40e2-9994-2ddb6d46c270"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "045bb5fb-b6bf-41f6-a758-57452dfae0ae",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "045bb5fb-b6bf-41f6-a758-57452dfae0ae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bdbd0fbb-1b85-48e0-a188-abdcbd93a73a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82ac5a38-2621-40b7-b74b-1672bbffaa42",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82ac5a38-2621-40b7-b74b-1672bbffaa42",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bdbd0fbb-1b85-48e0-a188-abdcbd93a73a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5060c5f4-8bd2-488b-b73f-ac58b143e397",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It's important to know the differences between binary and decimal number systems. As a review, the decimal number system is base-10. There are ten different symbols used to represent numbers (0-9). The binary numbers system is base-2. There are two different symbols used to represent numbers (0-1). Using our Flippy Do, we can convert between Binary and Decimal number systems.While it is easier for humans to use the decimal number system in our everyday lives, we will see later in this unit how electrical signals inside computers can be best represented by using the the binary number system.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5060c5f4-8bd2-488b-b73f-ac58b143e397",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bdbd0fbb-1b85-48e0-a188-abdcbd93a73a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93abecb4-f4c6-430f-a920-84f127453dd0",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Facilitate a discussion using the “3-2-1 Closing the Loop” protocol.\n- What are the three things you learned today?\n- What are two things you found interesting?\n- What is one question you still have?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** This closing routine surfaces what stuck and what didn't, without needing a single right answer. Listen for whether the three things learned include place value, the bit/byte definitions, or the idea that more bits allow more values to be represented. A lingering question is useful diagnostic information, not something to resolve on the spot."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93abecb4-f4c6-430f-a920-84f127453dd0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bdbd0fbb-1b85-48e0-a188-abdcbd93a73a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eef2fde4-0388-400b-a44c-99e40b2fa0aa",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eef2fde4-0388-400b-a44c-99e40b2fa0aa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bdbd0fbb-1b85-48e0-a188-abdcbd93a73a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "74196b77-0e5d-4907-a9bc-370ccd4c282d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Imagine you work at a local store. In the register all you have are nine $10 bills, nine $1 bills, and nine dimes.\n* What’s the largest amount of change that you can give someone?\n* What’s the least?\n* What would you do if someone needed 7 cents in change?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** The goal of the prompt is to understand the very real problem of making sure that enough place values are available to represent numbers. At both extremes of the number range, large and small -- and in-between numbers -- you are unable to build some numbers because you don’t have the place values to do so.\n* The largest amount of change you can give someone is $99.90.\n* The least change you can give is $0.10.\n* You can’t give someone $0.07. You also can’t give someone $1.25 in change (because you have no nickels!)\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "74196b77-0e5d-4907-a9bc-370ccd4c282d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "acc9c2f4-7109-467f-9f11-db2a21465045"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6532972c-5a5f-405b-9d3b-357a399295c3",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we’re going to explore what happens when you don’t have the right “places” to store information. This can happen when you try to store very large numbers, very small ones, and everything in-between! ",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6532972c-5a5f-405b-9d3b-357a399295c3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "acc9c2f4-7109-467f-9f11-db2a21465045"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ecad6773-5af5-4070-971e-7276515c50a1",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ecad6773-5af5-4070-971e-7276515c50a1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "acc9c2f4-7109-467f-9f11-db2a21465045"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "11cb0bec-8b86-4dfb-abb7-e4d0601aeded",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Discover what happens when a number system runs out of place values while counting up.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in groups of two. Groups will need one computer per group.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Odometer Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students will tackle the problem of “running out of place values” when counting to bigger and bigger numbers. They will play with a digital odometer widget to explore what happens when you add one to the largest number you can represent in a number system.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "11cb0bec-8b86-4dfb-abb7-e4d0601aeded",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4f8ba863-04b5-4426-a3db-fa00803c81fe",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**Have students navigate to Lesson 7, Level 1 and play with the odometer to figure out how it works. Explain to students that this is a widget that simulates a car odometer - a device that tracks how far the car has driven (in miles or kilometers). Have students explore the odometer to understand how it works.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Give students two to three minutes to explore the odometer.\n",
        "progression_name": "Widget: Binary Odometer"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4f8ba863-04b5-4426-a3db-fa00803c81fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ef3af082-2d28-4e9b-8aea-c73586fd9fe1",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now I'm going to give you a challenge to tackle with the Binary Odometer. Write your response on your scratch paper.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ef3af082-2d28-4e9b-8aea-c73586fd9fe1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92e618bc-f096-4bbc-90c8-064e7b0fdb22",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct students to set the binary odometer to the highest number possible. Then let it run!\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92e618bc-f096-4bbc-90c8-064e7b0fdb22",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b3ddd9f4-4048-4067-b957-ec3f666e4675",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to show the discussion prompts.\n* *What happens to the odometer reading?*\n* *Does the odometer still show the distance driven by the car?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** When the odometer turns over—exceeds the number of place values it can physically display—the odometer reading correctly shows all but one number in its place value positions. For example after the odometer turns over, all the numbers should flip to 0 and the one of the left should flip to 1. But we are out of place values! So the numbers flip to 0, but nothing flips to 1. The number we are trying to represent does not fit in the number of bits we have available to accurately display it.\n\nLike a bathtub that has reached its maximum capacity for holding water and is now overflowing, the odometer has reached its maximum capacity and has reached overflow.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b3ddd9f4-4048-4067-b957-ec3f666e4675",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7dcd2e0-24a9-4794-8d4c-ef59fc34b7d4",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define overflow error. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that some languages, like Python, don't limit whole numbers to a fixed number of place values. Python integers can grow as large as the computer's memory allows, so unlike the odometer or Flippy-Do, they don't hit an overflow error.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7dcd2e0-24a9-4794-8d4c-ef59fc34b7d4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4cc15ee8-4504-47dc-be69-f855bf4ebf31",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Why might a language want its integers to never overflow? What would a program need to be able to do to make that possible?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should land on the idea that overflow is a design tradeoff, not an unavoidable law of computing. A language that never overflows on integers has to give up the fixed, fast, hardware-sized storage that other languages use, and instead let numbers take up more memory as they grow. That tradeoff is worth it in contexts where an unexpected overflow (a counter silently resetting or going negative) would be dangerous or confusing, and worth avoiding in performance-critical contexts where speed and predictable memory use matter more.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4cc15ee8-4504-47dc-be69-f855bf4ebf31",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "20ab4640-df8e-4aba-b7e9-58d7d7bc4e1f",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 2 to complete the check for understanding and explain what happens to the odometer reading once it exceeds its maximum number of place values, and why the extra digit doesn't appear anywhere.\n",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding - Overflow",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 2 can be used as a formative assessment to check student understanding of the lesson objectives.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "20ab4640-df8e-4aba-b7e9-58d7d7bc4e1f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c3b924c7-e1c4-4d54-a63c-0304269cda24",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Recognize that adding bits extends the range of numbers you can represent, but any fixed number of bits still has an overflow point.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "More Flippy Do Practice"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c3b924c7-e1c4-4d54-a63c-0304269cda24",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "68414410-b82c-4508-ac65-3fcc7ff8c7c2",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now let's look at the limits of our Flippy Do when it comes to overflow.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "68414410-b82c-4508-ac65-3fcc7ff8c7c2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "96e63b19-8f30-4210-9f5f-b84bacc5281d",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:**  Click through the animated slide to show the discussion prompts.\n* *What value would cause your Flippy-Do to overflow?*\n* *What adaptation could you make to the Flippy-Do to represent that value?* \n* *Using your newly adapted Flippy-Do, how many total numbers can be represented?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:**  Use this conversation to show that we can always add bits to represent more numbers but when working with a set number of bits, at some point we will run into overflow if we try to represent large enough values.\n* Look for students to physically model overflow using the Flippy Do, going from when all of the 1s are flipped up to the next number causing all the 1s to reset to 0s.\n* With the Flippy Do, trying to represent the number 256 will cause an overflow. One common adaptation students will come up with is adding an additional column/bit to the Flippy Do to be able to represent that number. For a 9-bit Flippy Do, the first number that will cause overflow is 512.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "96e63b19-8f30-4210-9f5f-b84bacc5281d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc3387f5-c8ee-4edf-a393-dbe158606958",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that you have started thinking about place value and overflow, we are going to work on a different problem. What happens when there aren’t enough place values to represent a number?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc3387f5-c8ee-4edf-a393-dbe158606958",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d465e06-4d21-4ebc-9666-64c377e8894c",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of two. \n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "There is an optional half-page activity guide with this chart that you can provide to students, if desired. Many students may use traditional rounding to decide on the prices for each of the candies. Some may come up with a system where the shop pays the difference and others may come up with a system where the customer pays the difference. See the exemplar handout for how those values would be represented in binary.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d465e06-4d21-4ebc-9666-64c377e8894c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e23187e5-7ad3-49c7-9b60-5cad0f717267",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students work with their partner on setting the four prices for their candy shop."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e23187e5-7ad3-49c7-9b60-5cad0f717267",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dfb1dbf3-1f38-4ef1-b74d-7f71f1395c1f",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to show the discussion prompts.\n* *How did you go about deciding which binary number to use for each price?*\n* *In your system, who pays the difference between the true price and the value stored in your computer system: the shop owner or the customer?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Use this discussion to highlight that the Flippy Do doesn't have the precision needed to represent these fractional values and in that case, decisions must be made on how to represent those values. In this case, those decisions impact who ends up benefitting when it comes to the price of each candy. You could ask you students to compare different systems.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use the Retrieve-Pair-Share strategy to facilitate this discussion by directing students to write down their thoughts and ideas first, then pair up students to compare their answers and expand their notes. This allows time for individual processing and idea generation before sharing thoughts with others. Ask students to share the ideas they discussed with their partners as a class.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dfb1dbf3-1f38-4ef1-b74d-7f71f1395c1f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9aec8d4c-d1a6-436a-a39a-ce0da4b65813",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define round-off error. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use the slide to help students better understand round-off errors. Explain that the customers and shop owners are depending on a given amount of candy always having the same price. Computers rely on precision. Think of a calculator. We depend on 1+1 always equaling 2. Rounding is necessary because of the limits of bits, but can lead to errors.\n\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9aec8d4c-d1a6-436a-a39a-ce0da4b65813",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "38d593d6-c4c5-4eb7-9757-921dc0cfdde2",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What problems could come from having both of these two types of scales in single one of your stores?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Use this discussion to highlight the importance of precision. \n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "38d593d6-c4c5-4eb7-9757-921dc0cfdde2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ebf29458-55dc-4819-b19d-04ea8eaf47da",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 3 to complete the check for understanding and explain why binary, like decimal, can't represent every possible fraction exactly, and what happens to the ones it can't.",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 3 can be used as a formative assessment to check student understanding of the lesson objectives.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ebf29458-55dc-4819-b19d-04ea8eaf47da",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e3556df-339b-4d74-9a1d-409a9491000b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "45c77aac-e701-4913-8ecc-83590c2b14dd",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the information showing how binary can be used to precisely represent some fractional values from the decimal number system. Students do not need to understand how to convert fractional amounts from decimal to binary.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For your own personal knowledge, you can read more about \"decimal points\" and \"binary points\" on Wikipedia."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "45c77aac-e701-4913-8ecc-83590c2b14dd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7543032-da01-4c56-8318-4ae659f24207"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "71f69c96-1d39-4ecc-a290-4f039158cef6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The most important takeaway from this lesson is to understand that bits can represent a limited amount of information.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "71f69c96-1d39-4ecc-a290-4f039158cef6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7543032-da01-4c56-8318-4ae659f24207"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0cddfd7b-9bf5-48c3-95ed-804c1ca26606",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:**  Click through the animated slide to show the discussion prompts.\n* What does the binary odometer show about representing large numbers?\n* If we had a big enough odometer or Flippy Do, could we represent every possible number? \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:**  As students have seen, you can make “any number” in your head, but the tool you use to represent a number has limitations. It has a fixed number of place values it can show. The odometer keeps running after you move beyond its upper limit, but the largest place values cannot be displayed due to overflow error.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0cddfd7b-9bf5-48c3-95ed-804c1ca26606",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7543032-da01-4c56-8318-4ae659f24207"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "db3889e1-1663-41ec-8034-2cd6863c254c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Review the Key Vocabulary.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "db3889e1-1663-41ec-8034-2cd6863c254c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7543032-da01-4c56-8318-4ae659f24207"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fcd65a4a-39bb-406b-9b38-e87bf5bde53d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students work with a partner to brainstorm what they think this list represents. When students have an idea, have them come up with another item to add to this list and be ready to explain why it belongs. If students are stuck, you can give them a hint that the list secretly represents four things we experience every year and it is not complete - there are others that we could add to it. The goal here is to get students to recognize that while each item in the list represents a non-denominational holiday - New Years Day, Valentine’s Day, Independence Day, and Halloween, the numbers themselves can be used to represent different types of information.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Alternate Discussion:** This example could be adapted to fit your classroom or school environment in order to better help your students predict what the list represents. For example: a list of room numbers in your school that students can relate to, or a list of street addresses that have meaning to students. If you decide to adapt this example, make sure it still addresses the larger concept that this warm-up introduces: that numbers can be used to represent different types of information."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fcd65a4a-39bb-406b-9b38-e87bf5bde53d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "111da800-cdd3-45a3-a20c-ef3993a7a1d2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "27d32147-79aa-42f7-80b9-8ceb3eeae67e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "27d32147-79aa-42f7-80b9-8ceb3eeae67e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "111da800-cdd3-45a3-a20c-ef3993a7a1d2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8706dcde-6bbf-4120-8238-1c3921890f0a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8706dcde-6bbf-4120-8238-1c3921890f0a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "111da800-cdd3-45a3-a20c-ef3993a7a1d2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8d226ab6-8381-40ee-a2f9-d0e10aaea41d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Build and refine a working system for translating text into numbers, discovering firsthand why a shared standard is useful.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in pairs. Assign students to \"Partner A\" or \"Partner B\".\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a few sticky notes.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Representing Text"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8d226ab6-8381-40ee-a2f9-d0e10aaea41d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d80fc6a6-9e53-4a45-b383-a60fabafc64c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In our warm-up, we used numbers to represent certain type of information. In today's activity, we will also use numbers to represent something that computers use pretty frequently: text messages.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d80fc6a6-9e53-4a45-b383-a60fabafc64c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8f0f213d-389b-4708-925f-4fa9195d0740",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Go over today's challenge: Using only numbers, they will need to communicate messages like the examples on the slide to their partner. No letters or other characters (including spaces) allowed! Give students a minute to come up with a system that allows them to do this.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Most likely students will arrive at a system where they assigned each character to a corresponding number.\n\nFor example: a = 1, b = 2\n\nIf students are struggling, remind them to consider each character individually rather than the whole word at once."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8f0f213d-389b-4708-925f-4fa9195d0740",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "72bc23ba-0c22-4e4e-9f7f-a185a39abd8d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Challenge #1 - Spaces**\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct the appropriate partner to put their head down, then click the animated slide to display the message.\n- Partner A: Heads down!\n- Partner B: On a sticky note, use your system to write the message: oh no"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "72bc23ba-0c22-4e4e-9f7f-a185a39abd8d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d3b4e769-72f6-4c36-bed6-8b3e9d588d84",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct the appropriate partner to put their head down, then instruct the other partner to put their head up and translate the message on the sticky note using their system.\n- Partner A: Heads Up! Translate the message on the sticky note using your system.\n- Partner B: Heads down!"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d3b4e769-72f6-4c36-bed6-8b3e9d588d84",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "37c74fd6-5160-4d26-9d33-1be943270e87",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct both partners to put their heads up, then check their answers. Ensure students understand that the space between the two words, as well as lowercase vs uppercase letters matter here. After students have checked, allowed them time to update their system as needed."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "37c74fd6-5160-4d26-9d33-1be943270e87",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82b1a14d-fb9e-4f21-9107-f88c3254264b",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Challenge #2 - Punctuation**\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct the appropriate partner to put their head down, then click the animated slide to display the message.\n- Partner B: Heads down!\n- Partner A: On a sticky note, use your system to write the message: Great!"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82b1a14d-fb9e-4f21-9107-f88c3254264b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "42093f0e-7876-41f8-9e55-42f97dd2cf7f",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct the appropriate partner to put their head down, then instruct the other partner to put their head up and translate the message on the sticky note using their system.\n- Partner B: Heads Up! Translate the message on the sticky note using your system.\n- Partner A: Heads down!"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "42093f0e-7876-41f8-9e55-42f97dd2cf7f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "668eee1f-4004-481c-8eec-d14dd81cb5fa",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct both partners to put their heads up, then check their answers. After students have checked, allowed them time to update their system as needed."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "668eee1f-4004-481c-8eec-d14dd81cb5fa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9302c1d2-e527-4361-88e0-18149893215b",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Challenge #3 - Capitals and Numbers**\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct the appropriate partner to put their head down, then click the animated slide to display the message.\n- Partner A: Heads down!\n- Partner B: On a sticky note, use your system to write the message: Free at 5?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9302c1d2-e527-4361-88e0-18149893215b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c57fc2bb-0053-4986-90ac-6223149a1ce7",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct the appropriate partner to put their head down, then instruct the other partner to put their head up and translate the message on the sticky note using their system.\n- Partner A: Heads Up! Translate the message on the sticky note using your system.\n- Partner B: Heads down!"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c57fc2bb-0053-4986-90ac-6223149a1ce7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "49ce5aff-c32b-4c48-a0bd-f47343dc8c97",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct both partners to put their heads up, then check their answers. After students have checked, allowed them time to update their system as needed."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "49ce5aff-c32b-4c48-a0bd-f47343dc8c97",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8663e234-4475-4176-b75a-e0b46a4b6d4e",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Challenge #4 - Review All Symbols**\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct the appropriate partner to put their head down, then click the animated slide to display the message.\n- Partner B: Heads down!\n- Partner A: On a sticky note, use your system to write the message: Cya 2nite!"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8663e234-4475-4176-b75a-e0b46a4b6d4e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "823fa4d3-d64e-4e5d-9e89-ce350bb13bd5",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct the appropriate partner to put their head down, then instruct the other partner to put their head up and translate the message on the sticky note using their system.\n- Partner B: Heads Up! Translate the message on the sticky note using your system.\n- Partner A: Heads down!"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "823fa4d3-d64e-4e5d-9e89-ce350bb13bd5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "39ca88c0-46b5-4acd-9de6-bda8b3ed9aba",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct both partners to put their heads up, then check their answers. After students have checked, allowed them time to update their system as needed."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "39ca88c0-46b5-4acd-9de6-bda8b3ed9aba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "725c4d17-38ee-4b86-be06-798013ac8677",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Direct students to find another group and discuss the following prompts before discussing with the class:\n- *Compare your systems. How are they the same? How are they different?*\n- *What's the minimum number of bits each of your systems would need per character? How do you know?*\n- *Hint: Convert your largest number in your system into binary. How many bits does this number require?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** There are many possible systems and there's usually not a \"right\" one so long as everyone agrees.\n- Adding more possibilities increases the risk of having too many characters to represent and not enough bits to do it. This is a practical example of the overflow and rounding issues from the previous lesson.\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The four challenges are designed to test the limits of the students' systems. For each challenge, after one partner has put their head down, click through to reveal the challenge to the the other partner.\n\nAfter the partner has translated the message to numbers, they hand the sticky note to the other partner and put their head down. The other partner translates back to English.\n\nAfter the challenge is done, give students a few minutes to update their systems as needed.\n\nBe on the lookout for cheats in a system - students cannot use spaces (aside from between two numbers to differentiate that where one number stops and the other starts) or symbols - only numbers one after the other."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "725c4d17-38ee-4b86-be06-798013ac8677",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6a346f1-6717-49ec-80f6-84910400c4d0",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Goal of this activity: Compare a real, standardized encoding to the systems students just invented, and see that ASCII solves the same problem they just solved.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Introducing ASCII"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6a346f1-6717-49ec-80f6-84910400c4d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eaa6f464-a0a0-49b2-ba81-7f6848ffb002",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You just invented your own scheme for representing text with numbers. Given we use text on our phones and computers all the time, we should assume there is a standard representation for most of the symbols you can type on an American keyboard. Today we're going to be looking at one called or ASCII (pronounced: “Ask-ee”) or the American Standard Code for Information Interchange",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eaa6f464-a0a0-49b2-ba81-7f6848ffb002",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b2b1516-d53a-472c-818b-a1e280c669fd",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a copy of the ASCII Student Reference  (optional) or project the reference sheet for students to see."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b2b1516-d53a-472c-818b-a1e280c669fd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ddf4e3f-916c-4c16-b371-59de9520cd00",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What's the same as the systems you created?*\n- *What's different?*\n- *What is most interesting or surprising about this system?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Briefly discuss these prompts as a class. At this point the main features of the system should be familiar but use this discussion to reinforce previous points.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**How familiar with ASCII?:** Students should be aware that ASCII exists and see how its development mirrors their own experiences in the previous activity, but they are not required to memorize the ASCII table. If your students are able to quickly recognize the point of the ASCII table feel free to move on to the wrap up."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ddf4e3f-916c-4c16-b371-59de9520cd00",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "68a0e7fd-0185-47d3-878c-32432a56d765",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that ASCII works great if all you ever need is English letters, numbers, and punctuation. But text messages today include way more than that: other languages, emoji, symbols. ASCII's 8 bits just aren't enough room for all of it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "68a0e7fd-0185-47d3-878c-32432a56d765",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "738fb1db-1d80-478e-9a8c-a3d294a27916",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What would you have to do to your own system if you suddenly needed to represent 1,000 different characters instead of 100?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should connect this back to slide 21's overflow reasoning: more characters to represent means you eventually need more bits, whether that's their own system, ASCII, or Unicode. Unicode is the real-world solution: it uses a variable number of bits (as many as needed) instead of ASCII's fixed 8, which is exactly the kind of fixed-vs-flexible tradeoff they've been running into all lesson."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "738fb1db-1d80-478e-9a8c-a3d294a27916",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "27d145f1-e4c1-4296-8076-615e752c6ddd",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *abstraction*. Explain that sending text messages is an example of abstraction. For a computer, each character is really just a number, which itself is really a binary number. It all comes back to zeroes and ones!"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "27d145f1-e4c1-4296-8076-615e752c6ddd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b751e7d6-26a0-4ab0-aeb3-f672d3829106",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What is another example of an abstraction from your everyday life? Something where you don’t completely understand how it works but you can still use it with confidence?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Student answers will vary. Examples: driving a car, turning on the lights in your room, riding in an elevator, etc."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b751e7d6-26a0-4ab0-aeb3-f672d3829106",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "425592bb-ace1-4f14-b59d-53c929fd2b58",
      "position": 25,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 1 to complete the check for understanding questions on levels 1 and 3.\n* **Level 1:** Students explain whether or not a certain binary pattern always represents a certain number.\n* **Level 2:** Students explain the challenge of representing an expanding set of characters (like emoji or other languages) using a fixed number of bits per character.\n* **Level 3:** Students explain what abstraction hides when someone sends a text message, and why that's useful.\n",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 1 can be used as a formative assessment to check student understanding of the lesson objectives."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "425592bb-ace1-4f14-b59d-53c929fd2b58",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81ff1bd9-7958-46d6-ab63-5f98ef668971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2092f8c6-6b60-4d6a-847f-1a4a96e72fe2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2092f8c6-6b60-4d6a-847f-1a4a96e72fe2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9ed72e87-f3be-43fc-95e9-ab99ae6f3be8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a5b75ca-b158-49a1-8577-12aa0a4a73c5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a5b75ca-b158-49a1-8577-12aa0a4a73c5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9ed72e87-f3be-43fc-95e9-ab99ae6f3be8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd2ce5b7-69af-427f-84c7-fb23ef384953",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Representing text is an example of abstraction, which is when we create simplified representations of something more complex. This lets us hide the details and instead focus on problems at a higher level.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd2ce5b7-69af-427f-84c7-fb23ef384953",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9ed72e87-f3be-43fc-95e9-ab99ae6f3be8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ea9e2a73-f5ab-4c5e-b4bf-25e39344802d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What problem were we trying to solve today?*\n- *What details from the last few lessons were hidden or out of focus?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify they were trying to send text messages. Students were using decimal numbers instead of binary numbers or AB patterns.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use the Retrieve-Pair-Share strategy to facilitate this discussion by directing students to write down their thoughts and ideas first, then pair up students to compare their answers and expand their notes. This allows time for individual processing and idea generation before sharing thoughts with others. Ask students to share the ideas they discussed with their partners as a class."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ea9e2a73-f5ab-4c5e-b4bf-25e39344802d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9ed72e87-f3be-43fc-95e9-ab99ae6f3be8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "10d51988-1ece-4729-b40e-f94f1cfd5e3f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "10d51988-1ece-4729-b40e-f94f1cfd5e3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9ed72e87-f3be-43fc-95e9-ab99ae6f3be8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1413b821-d20d-4f80-821d-6bfd84506e51",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How could a picture be turned into a list of 0s and 1s?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** The goal here is that students suggest splitting the picture into squares and giving each square a 0 or a 1. You can draw out the need for an agreed order and an agreed meaning of 0 and 1 while they use the widget.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use the Retrieve-Pair-Share strategy to facilitate this discussion by directing students to write down their thoughts and ideas first, then pair up students to compare their answers and expand their notes. This allows time for individual processing and idea generation before sharing thoughts with others. Ask students to share the ideas they discussed with their partners as a class."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1413b821-d20d-4f80-821d-6bfd84506e51",
        "lesson_activity.key": "058aa0bc-1745-478e-a70b-dffdf5a2e558"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e7517d1e-3410-4fb6-a3b6-5194b674a52d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e7517d1e-3410-4fb6-a3b6-5194b674a52d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "058aa0bc-1745-478e-a70b-dffdf5a2e558"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a0a19185-69d8-455e-92bc-baeda9bb09ad",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a0a19185-69d8-455e-92bc-baeda9bb09ad",
        "lesson_activity.key": "058aa0bc-1745-478e-a70b-dffdf5a2e558"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "70d55a80-b671-44fd-9eec-db9bf76c7339",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Learn how the pixelation widget turns each square of a picture into a bit, and practice entering and fixing bits to build an image.\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Show the \"Pixelation Widget\" video.\n\n**Questions to consider with the video:**\n- How does the widget use bits to make a black and white image?",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Intro to the Pixelation Widget",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The video displays an old version of the Pixelation Widget. In the version your students will use, they no longer need to set the width and height using binary numbers - they can use the slider at the top. They may also note that the width and height is not displayed in their work space in binary numbers.\n\nAlso, while the video uses the word \"metadata\" to describe this information, students do not need to leave this lesson understanding metadata. Metadata will be covered in a later lesson."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "70d55a80-b671-44fd-9eec-db9bf76c7339",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a896032f-052e-4a89-9bbd-78b726bb890a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "be9f1358-6a25-4608-81e1-30c4c0e911b5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 1 to practice using the Pixelation widget.\n- **Level 1:** Students will recreate the letter A using the black and white Pixelation Widget. They will first use the sliders to set the width and height of the image. Then, they will type the appropriate bit for each portion of the image (\"0\" for black, \"1\" for white).\n- **Level 2:** Students must find and delete the extra bit that is causing the image to be distorted.\n",
        "progression_name": "Pixelation Widget",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Getting Familiar with the Pixelation Widget:** Once most of the class has finished Levels 1 and 2, you can move on. Encourage students who are finished to help those who haven't. The point of these two levels is to get students familiar with the tool and practice entering the bits."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "be9f1358-6a25-4608-81e1-30c4c0e911b5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a896032f-052e-4a89-9bbd-78b726bb890a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5b5df080-c361-4888-8373-aaeb5955c6fb",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Practice turning each square of a picture into a bit, and notice how the number of bits changes the picture.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Building Digital Images "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5b5df080-c361-4888-8373-aaeb5955c6fb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a896032f-052e-4a89-9bbd-78b726bb890a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f80a39a8-527b-451e-a75a-b2d10e71d920",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that you've seen how to set each pixel black or white, you'll build two versions of the same picture. In Challenge A you'll use big squares and decide, one square at a time, whether it should be black or white. In Challenge B the squares are smaller. As you work, keep track of how many bits you need and how the picture changes. Try it out yourself!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f80a39a8-527b-451e-a75a-b2d10e71d920",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a896032f-052e-4a89-9bbd-78b726bb890a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe3724fc-d7cd-48d8-afbe-385b55099435",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a copy of the Black and White Images Activity Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe3724fc-d7cd-48d8-afbe-385b55099435",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a896032f-052e-4a89-9bbd-78b726bb890a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "249e28e7-6266-4cc2-9d12-108fda81f802",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 3, then Level 4. Students build each picture and answer the Activity Guide questions.",
        "progression_name": "Challenges A & B",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Questions to Ask as You Circulate:** Having students work from paper copies of the Activity Guide makes it easier to read each square and transcribe the bits into the widget. As students work, circulate the room and ask pairs of students questions about their process:\n\n* How are you deciding whether each square should be black or white?\n* Do you think this process will give a good picture? Why or why not?\n* What would you change to make the picture look more like the original?"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "249e28e7-6266-4cc2-9d12-108fda81f802",
