    {
      "key": "10af0c47-a463-4529-a959-83ff4d0a794d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "10af0c47-a463-4529-a959-83ff4d0a794d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68bee37-80cc-4a95-955e-e1818b97ea53"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "da615333-e22c-482f-87ac-8f039a95330c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "da615333-e22c-482f-87ac-8f039a95330c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68bee37-80cc-4a95-955e-e1818b97ea53"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d6ec7271-0144-4909-bedc-e9e975a310b2",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the widget, you worked directly with bits to make colors. Someone looking at a photo on a phone gets those colors without ever seeing a 0 or a 1. Something sits between the bits and the color.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d6ec7271-0144-4909-bedc-e9e975a310b2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68bee37-80cc-4a95-955e-e1818b97ea53"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e3b8c1b2-0d5f-48a7-a733-9904e8a8edac",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *When you made colors in the widget, what did you have to work with?*\n- *When someone views a photo on a phone, what do they never have to think about?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that they worked directly with bits in the widget, while a person viewing an image does not have to. Layers of abstraction make that possible by hiding details that are not needed for a given purpose. Move on once students name that the bits are hidden from the viewer."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e3b8c1b2-0d5f-48a7-a733-9904e8a8edac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68bee37-80cc-4a95-955e-e1818b97ea53"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0e0a1826-86e9-46e3-8a6c-8d364b2e563f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation. As each layer appears, ask students what that layer lets someone ignore. Then direct students to record the layers of abstraction in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0e0a1826-86e9-46e3-8a6c-8d364b2e563f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68bee37-80cc-4a95-955e-e1818b97ea53"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "08c28601-a77b-4ada-be37-0d41bc7acf04",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A digital image is made up of several layers that work together to turn bits into color. This is abstraction. Each layer hides details that the layer above it does not need, which is why you can look at a photo without thinking about bits. Let's watch a video of how this works.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "08c28601-a77b-4ada-be37-0d41bc7acf04",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68bee37-80cc-4a95-955e-e1818b97ea53"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "76ac5972-2424-4095-a172-8ee63d02ae37",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Show the How Computers Work Video."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "76ac5972-2424-4095-a172-8ee63d02ae37",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68bee37-80cc-4a95-955e-e1818b97ea53"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5928e1fb-b06b-4812-88f7-42ba07f38070",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine you have to teach a computer to spot fish in the ocean, but the only way you can tell it anything is by writing rules. Make your rules specific enough that a computer could follow them with no guessing.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5928e1fb-b06b-4812-88f7-42ba07f38070",
        "lesson_activity.key": "43eb0e5f-e522-485e-8d1f-c5aa8bc683d3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a298b121-4892-4e35-ba59-d042e36b81a9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students write three rules on scratch paper. Give them 2 minutes to write silently, then 1 minute to compare with a neighbor."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a298b121-4892-4e35-ba59-d042e36b81a9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "43eb0e5f-e522-485e-8d1f-c5aa8bc683d3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aedf8035-835b-4583-88d1-705c3c14dcbf",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal the pictures and give students 2 minutes to check each one against their three rules.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Which pictures broke your rules?*\n- *Did any rule accept something that is not a fish, or reject a real fish?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that rules written by people miss cases nobody planned for, and that fixing one rule can break another. Many students' rules will overlap (lives in water, has fins, swims), which shows how a few shared assumptions can all fail on the same unexpected picture.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students say the rules just need to be more detailed, ask, \"What rule would you add for the dolphin? Would it break for any real fish?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aedf8035-835b-4583-88d1-705c3c14dcbf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "43eb0e5f-e522-485e-8d1f-c5aa8bc683d3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a98ee845-fc70-44ef-a02d-a946bb1229af",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a98ee845-fc70-44ef-a02d-a946bb1229af",
        "lesson_activity.key": "43eb0e5f-e522-485e-8d1f-c5aa8bc683d3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe5b0a72-476f-42bb-bfe0-06991583b545",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe5b0a72-476f-42bb-bfe0-06991583b545",
        "lesson_activity.key": "43eb0e5f-e522-485e-8d1f-c5aa8bc683d3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bab563d0-2ade-4b41-93e1-9ca43a651be5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students train an A.I. with their own labels so they experience how a model learns from examples, then give them the vocabulary to describe it: AI system, model, and the difference between rules-based and learned behavior.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Instruct students to take out their copy of the CSP Unit Guide.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Train the AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bab563d0-2ade-4b41-93e1-9ca43a651be5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93754f08-7aa2-4d6a-96b8-ca8ecdecafe0",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Play the video \"What is Machine Learning\".",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Alternatives to AI For Oceans:** AI for Oceans was originally developed as an Hour of Code activity that can be completed by students with any device available. We have modified it for its usage here. Depending on your classroom situation, you might opt to replace the activity with:\n\n**Teachable Machine** - *Teachable Machine is a web-based tool that makes creating machine learning models fast, easy, and accessible to everyone. Teachable Machine is flexible – use files or capture examples live. It’s respectful of the way you work. You can even choose to use it entirely on-device, without any webcam or microphone data leaving your computer.*\n\nIf your classrooms devices have cameras, Teachable Machines offers an engaging way to create training sets. Encourage students to teach the machine to represent rock, paper, or scissors with hand gestures. What are some possible ways for bias to enter in?\n\n**Machine Learning for Kids** - *This free tool introduces machine learning by providing hands-on experiences for training machine learning systems and building things with them. It provides an easy-to-use guided environment for training machine learning models to recognise text, numbers, images, or sounds.*\n\nMachine Learning for Kids is a great option if your students want to work with text samples. Teach the machine to recognize words or passages that are happy or sad. Lots to play around with here!"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93754f08-7aa2-4d6a-96b8-ca8ecdecafe0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f9c23f04-b6a2-4918-8043-99d80dedbd6a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Machine learning refers to a computer that can recognize patterns and make decisions. In this activity you’re going to supply the data to train your own machine learning model. Imagine an ocean that contains creatures like fish, but also contains trash dumped by humans. What if we could train a computer to tell the difference and then use that technology to help clean the ocean?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f9c23f04-b6a2-4918-8043-99d80dedbd6a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e5091401-bdef-4018-8949-d0ffd49fd294",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1-3. Students should spend around five minutes total on these levels.\n- **Level 1:** Students train the AI to recognize \"fish\" or \"not fish\"\n- **Level 2:** Students see what happens when their training from level 1 is used to determine if other sea creatures belong in the ocean.\n- **Level 3:** Students train the AI on the broader question of what belongs in the water.\n\n",
        "progression_name": "Train the AI",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Prompt their thinking with the question \"What potential problems do you think come up when creating training sets?\""
          },
          {
            "type": "contentCorner",
            "markdown": "Every image in this part of the tutorial is fed into a neural network that has been pre-trained on a huge set of data called ImageNet. The database contains over 14 million hand-annotated images. ImageNet contains more than 20,000 categories with a typical category, such as \"balloon\" or \"strawberry\", consisting of several hundred images. When A.I. is scanning new images and making its own predictions in the tutorial, it is actually comparing the possible categories for the new image with the patterns it found in the training dataset."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e5091401-bdef-4018-8949-d0ffd49fd294",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "12a7b9c5-080a-4d6c-926d-1fcf47bfc491",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *How well did A.I. do?*\n- *How do you think it decided what to include in the ocean?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the A.I. only knew what their labels taught it. Answers about how well it did will vary, so the aim is to connect the results to the examples they supplied."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "12a7b9c5-080a-4d6c-926d-1fcf47bfc491",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "14b9fd6b-fa59-4a10-a93a-fb36902d0107",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Play the video \"Training Data & Bias\"."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "14b9fd6b-fa59-4a10-a93a-fb36902d0107",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a54b3f3f-dbf6-4ecc-ac89-4efc38f60367",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How do you think your training data influenced the results that A.I. produced?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the A.I. could only learn from the examples they gave it. When the training data left out sea creatures, the A.I. called them \"not fish,\" so students' choices as trainers shaped what it got right and wrong."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a54b3f3f-dbf6-4ecc-ac89-4efc38f60367",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc52740d-f39d-4c46-8ba0-42b2a63e0f10",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The fish-finder you just trained is an AI system. It took in pictures and produced a decision: fish or not fish.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc52740d-f39d-4c46-8ba0-42b2a63e0f10",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6ffa65b1-3fae-41fc-8fcd-2f5b2be17b67",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *AI System*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6ffa65b1-3fae-41fc-8fcd-2f5b2be17b67",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "633d3e03-03a0-488b-bc81-e2b6a4df33e6",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Inside the fish-finder, the model is the part that looks at a picture and decides. Your warm-up rules were one kind of model. The one you trained with labels is another kind.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "633d3e03-03a0-488b-bc81-e2b6a4df33e6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c41c7e99-ff94-47c7-aff2-7a4c02390ffa",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *model*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c41c7e99-ff94-47c7-aff2-7a4c02390ffa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c58b3a86-5981-472b-a9ed-2ece10063a7f",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Remember when the trained A.I. called sea creatures \"not fish\"? It had no examples of them. That is the learned version of a rule nobody wrote.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c58b3a86-5981-472b-a9ed-2ece10063a7f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82ba69c1-a4b1-4ca4-a028-9b4c39b9574c",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the table using students' own warm-up rules and the Levels 1-3 results.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Learned models still depend on human choices, because people pick the examples and the labels. That sets up the bias discussion that comes next."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82ba69c1-a4b1-4ca4-a028-9b4c39b9574c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f05eee2e-0266-479d-ba3b-7a792f8c761f",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students train an A.I. on a word that depends on opinion so they see their own judgments become the training data. Help them connect that experience to real computing innovations and to what designers and developers can do to reduce bias and consider the effects of their programs.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Bias in Training Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f05eee2e-0266-479d-ba3b-7a792f8c761f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "77ee3044-6927-469d-9cf1-ba00e66cfb34",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you will teach A.I. about a word of your choosing by showing it examples of that type of fish. As before, A.I. doesn't start with any training data about these labels. Even though the words in this level are fairly objective, it's possible that you will end up with different results based on their training data. You might even intentionally train A.I. incorrectly to see what happens!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "77ee3044-6927-469d-9cf1-ba00e66cfb34",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2dae29af-dc95-4f7b-8fd5-c2f4ebd38a23",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 4-5. Students should spend around five minutes total on these levels.\n- **Level 4:** Students train the AI to recognize a new word of their choice.\n- **Level 5:** Students train the AI on a new, subjective word of their choice.\n\n",
        "progression_name": "Bias In Training Data",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Prompt their thinking with the question \"Is it fair to use AI to judge something by looks? What unintended bias could this cause?\" Here, as before, students will use training data to teach A.I. to recognize different types of fish. The words in this list are intentionally more subjective than what students will have seen so far. Encourage students to decide for themselves what makes a fish look \"angry\" or \"fun\". Two students may choose the same label and get a very different set of results based on which fish traits were their focus. Encourage students to discuss their findings with each other or go back and choose new words. Each student will rely on their own opinions to train A.I. which means that A.I. will learn with the same biases held by the students. As students begin to see the role their opinion is playing, ask them to reflect on whether this is good or bad, and how it might be addressed."
          },
          {
            "type": "contentCorner",
            "markdown": "The fish in this tutorial are randomly generated based on some pre-defined components, including mouths, tails, eyes, scales, and fins, with a randomly chosen body color, shape, and size. Rather than looking at the actual image data, A.I. is now looking for patterns in these components based on how the student classifies each fish. It will be more likely to label a fish the same way the student would have if it has matching traits."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2dae29af-dc95-4f7b-8fd5-c2f4ebd38a23",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a4ff22cb-b448-488e-8c4d-d8e3cae51685",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-video\"></i> **Video:** Play the video \"How I’m Fighting Bias in Algorithms\" with Joy Buolamwini.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This clip stops before Joy's recommendations. If students ask what can be done about algorithmic bias, share her three ideas: who codes matters (diverse teams who can check each other's blind spots), how we code matters (building fairness into the system), and why we code matters (making social change a priority)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a4ff22cb-b448-488e-8c4d-d8e3cae51685",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ffa031a3-b212-476b-a2e6-564e5b46488f",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *How can computing innovations which make use of Machine Learning reflect existing human bias?*\n- *How could it be used to discriminate against groups of individuals?*\n- *How can that bias be minimized?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that machine learning can reflect human bias because people choose the training data and write the algorithms, and that collecting more data does not remove bias. They should begin to suggest ways to reduce it, such as more diverse training examples."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ffa031a3-b212-476b-a2e6-564e5b46488f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c0c4c33-bfb9-44d7-bf12-f0b62b1b1410",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 6 to answer the checking for understanding question.",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this level to check whether students can explain how training data can lead to unfair results, recognize that the app learns from examples instead of following rules, and describe a step designers can take to reduce bias before launch."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c0c4c33-bfb9-44d7-bf12-f0b62b1b1410",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b44f67c-f546-4dd3-9bcc-2d39a5d72ec4",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b44f67c-f546-4dd3-9bcc-2d39a5d72ec4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bd3f09-94ae-4289-b746-88a469e1c594"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7b788fa-9df6-4348-816e-ed479fa5e2d6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7b788fa-9df6-4348-816e-ed479fa5e2d6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ffb352ba-d9e3-4c1f-85ca-e3aca06621c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "34f1e773-dfbc-436e-a43d-9e7dbbf15268",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "34f1e773-dfbc-436e-a43d-9e7dbbf15268",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ffb352ba-d9e3-4c1f-85ca-e3aca06621c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ce2b85d7-679d-4005-a3c9-677ecfc2eba3",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *A spam filter deletes any email that contains the word \"free.\" What is the model in this AI system, and is it rules-based or learned? How do you know?*\n- *How would the filter change if it learned from emails that people marked as spam?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the model is the part of the filter that decides which emails to delete, and that the first filter is rules-based because people wrote the rule. Students should recognize that the second filter is learned because it finds patterns in examples, and that each kind fails in its own way: the rule misses messages nobody wrote a rule for, while the learned filter depends on which examples it was given.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students say the learned filter is simply better, ask, \"What if the emails people marked as spam were incomplete or unfair?\" This connects back to training data and bias from the activity."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ce2b85d7-679d-4005-a3c9-677ecfc2eba3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ffb352ba-d9e3-4c1f-85ca-e3aca06621c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "15e28f78-356c-4a8b-9646-f5e6e273974e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "15e28f78-356c-4a8b-9646-f5e6e273974e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ffb352ba-d9e3-4c1f-85ca-e3aca06621c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "569da64d-71a1-41c6-98ea-fcebd1e119b2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover next word prediction by doing it themselves, before generative AI or LLMs are named.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a set of sentence starter cards.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students in pairs or small groups. One student draws a sentence starter card and reads it aloud (e.g. \"The weather today is...\"). Without discussing, everyone else silently writes the word they think comes next."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "569da64d-71a1-41c6-98ea-fcebd1e119b2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c6094a87-79fb-4e2d-9fb3-f192dada6fea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a131cf2-d54d-4510-bb81-af5bcee14912",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What word did each person write?*\n- *Where did you agree? Where did you disagree*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students notice agreement on predictable starters and disagreement on ambiguous ones. Some words are simply more likely to come next than others.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students describe their guesses as \"obvious\" or \"random,\" that is the vocabulary you want. Reuse \"likely\" and \"pattern\" when you introduce generative AI next."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a131cf2-d54d-4510-bb81-af5bcee14912",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c6094a87-79fb-4e2d-9fb3-f192dada6fea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "36888e4c-5ed9-4142-8edc-f7495950bef7",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "36888e4c-5ed9-4142-8edc-f7495950bef7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c6094a87-79fb-4e2d-9fb3-f192dada6fea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "48d6ee54-84bf-4986-a0c2-1aa63838f13b",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "48d6ee54-84bf-4986-a0c2-1aa63838f13b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c6094a87-79fb-4e2d-9fb3-f192dada6fea"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a791822b-9aa3-49fd-bea0-133b44e683be",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Practice applying the concept of disclosure to real examples, and notice that not every case has an obvious answer.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *generative AI*.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Disclosure Voting"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a791822b-9aa3-49fd-bea0-133b44e683be",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "34be3888-402d-4166-8a22-a4ce97832609",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that LLMs generate text by predicting likely word sequences, the same thing students just did in the warm up."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "34be3888-402d-4166-8a22-a4ce97832609",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b4a583f1-c652-410e-a0c5-4356e3fcd15e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce that publishing content involves more than creating it. Creators must consider whether AI contributed, whether attribution is required, what license governs the content, and whether permission is needed before sharing."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b4a583f1-c652-410e-a0c5-4356e3fcd15e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5aff6597-23c5-497f-a270-b911f70ed00d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c5cce912-c340-4b21-b454-e844db7b9f32",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** For each example, give students about 30 seconds to vote. Have them defend their reasoning with a partner before discussing as a class.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep the pace brisk, about 30 seconds per example. Save deeper debate for the whole class discussion at the end."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c5cce912-c340-4b21-b454-e844db7b9f32",
